From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000008 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: PyDevices <pydevices@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 25 Aug 2026 13:00:00 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] py/lexer: Don't prime the input queue with the EOF sentinel

v1.28.0's 617c7dba3 changed MP_LEXER_EOF from (unichar)-1 to '\0', but
mp_lexer_new still primes chr0/1/2 with 0 - now indistinguishable from
EOF. For EMPTY input, next_char()'s end-of-file newline injection tests
chr1 against MP_LEXER_EOF while chr1 still holds a dummy, concludes it is
already past EOF, and never injects the trailing newline. single_input
then sees a bare MP_TOKEN_END and raises SyntaxError:

    compile('', '<stdin>', 'single')   # SyntaxError on 1.28, OK on 1.27

User-visible: pressing Enter on an empty line at any event-driven REPL
(webassembly, esp8266, ...) prints "SyntaxError: invalid syntax", since
pyexec_friendly_repl_process_char executes the empty line. CPython
accepts empty single input. Non-empty and whitespace-only lines are
unaffected, which is why this hid so well.

Prime the queue with MP_LEXER_INVALID_BYTE instead: like the old 0-vs-(-1)
pairing, it cannot be mistaken for EOF, and the dummies are consumed by
the three next_char() calls in mp_lexer_new before tokenization starts.

Applies cleanly to current upstream master, which has the same bug.

diff --git a/py/lexer.c b/py/lexer.c
index 22ff4d2cd..49e4d1410 100644
--- a/py/lexer.c
+++ b/py/lexer.c
@@ -1077,8 +1077,14 @@ mp_lexer_t *mp_lexer_new(qstr src_name, mp_reader_t reader) {
     lex->indent_level[0] = 0;
 
     // load lexer with start of file, advancing lex->column to 1
-    // start with dummy bytes and use next_char() for proper EOL/EOF handling
-    lex->chr0 = lex->chr1 = lex->chr2 = 0;
+    // Start with dummy bytes and use next_char() for proper EOL/EOF handling.
+    // The dummy must not be MP_LEXER_EOF ('\0'): the end-of-file newline
+    // injection in next_char() tests chr1 against it to decide whether a
+    // newline is still owed, and for empty input chr1 holds a dummy at that
+    // moment.  A zero dummy reads as "already at EOF", the newline is never
+    // injected, and empty input fails to parse as single_input (a regression
+    // from when the EOF sentinel was -1 and the zero dummy was distinct).
+    lex->chr0 = lex->chr1 = lex->chr2 = MP_LEXER_INVALID_BYTE;
     next_char(lex);
     next_char(lex);
     next_char(lex);
